### Definition of the List Contract and ArrayList Internals

The **`List<E>`** interface represents an ordered collection where the developer has precise control over where each element is inserted and accessed by its integer index.

**`ArrayList`** is the standard dynamic array implementation of the `List` interface. Internally, `ArrayList` stores its elements in a raw Object array:
```java
transient Object[] elementData; // Internal backing array
```

**Core Properties:**
* **`DEFAULT_CAPACITY`**: When an `ArrayList` is created with `new ArrayList<>()`, its initial internal capacity defaults to **`DEFAULT_CAPACITY`** (which is 10) once the first element is added.
* **Separation of Size vs Capacity**: `size()` is the number of actual elements currently stored. Capacity is the length of the internal `elementData` buffer.

---

### The 1.5x Expansion Formula and Growth Mechanics

When the backing array becomes full, `ArrayList` automatically allocates a new larger array and transfers existing elements:

$$\text{newCapacity} = \text{oldCapacity} + (\text{oldCapacity} \gg 1)$$

In the JDK source code, the expression **`oldCapacity + (oldCapacity >> 1)`** performs a bitwise right-shift (dividing by 2) and adds it to the old capacity, expanding the buffer by **50% (1.5x)**.

| Resize Step | Old Capacity | Growth (+50%) | New Capacity |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Initial** | 0 | First `add()` | 10 |
| **Growth 1** | 10 | $10 + 5$ | 15 |
| **Growth 2** | 15 | $15 + 7$ | 22 |
| **Growth 3** | 22 | $22 + 11$ | 33 |

---

### ArrayList in Code: Performance and Pre-Allocation

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class ArrayListInternalsDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Standard initialization (default capacity 10 upon first insert)
        ArrayList<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
        list.add("Item 1");
        list.add("Item 2");

        // High-performance pre-allocation: eliminates resizing overhead
        int estimatedSize = 100_000;
        ArrayList<Integer> fastList = new ArrayList<>(estimatedSize);
        // Alternatively, use ensureCapacity() on an existing list:
        fastList.ensureCapacity(estimatedSize);

        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            fastList.add(i * 10);
        }

        System.out.println("FastList size: " + fastList.size());
        System.out.println("Element at index 3: " + fastList.get(3));
    }
}
```

**Console Output:**
```text
FastList size: 5
Element at index 3: 30
```

---

### Time Complexity Guarantees

* **Random Access (`get(i)`, `set(i)` )**: $O(1)$ constant time (direct memory address calculation).
* **Append (`add(e)`)**: Amortized $O(1)$ constant time ($O(N)$ only during buffer resize).
* **Positional Insert/Delete (`add(i, e)`, `remove(i)`)**: $O(N)$ linear time (requires shifting subsequent elements).

