practiceProblems.problemStatement:
A developer wrote a loop to remove all words starting with 'Z' from an ArrayList:
```java
for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++) {
    if (list.get(i).startsWith("Z")) {
        list.remove(i);
    }
}
```
Bug: When consecutive 'Z' words appear (e.g. ["Zebra", "Zenith"]), deleting "Zebra" shifts "Zenith" into index 0, but the loop increments `i` to 1, completely skipping "Zenith"!

Your Task:
1. Fix the deletion logic by iterating **backwards** from `list.size() - 1` down to 0.
2. In `Main.main()`, test with: `["Apple", "Zebra", "Zenith", "Banana", "Zero", "Cherry"]`.
3. Print the cleaned list: `[Apple, Banana, Cherry]`

practiceProblems.solutionCode:
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> words = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList("Apple", "Zebra", "Zenith", "Banana", "Zero", "Cherry"));

        // Safe backward iteration
        for (int i = words.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            if (words.get(i).startsWith("Z")) {
                words.remove(i);
            }
        }

        System.out.println(words);
    }
}

